# PART 6 — SELECTION, INPUT AND UNIT CONTROLS

Selection reliability is a high-priority gameplay requirement.

Inspect the existing pointer handling, entity hit testing, selected-entity state, command system, camera controls and React event handling before making changes.

## 6.1 Selecting units

- Clicking a villager selects that villager.
- Clicking a soldier selects that soldier.
- Show a clear selection ring or outline.
- Display the selected unit's name, role, health, current activity and available commands.
- Clicking another unit switches selection to that unit.
- Do not cause the camera to jump unexpectedly when selecting an entity.
- Ensure the correct topmost interactive entity is selected when objects overlap.

## 6.2 Deselecting and inspecting entities

- Clicking genuinely empty, walkable terrain deselects the current unit in selection mode.
- Remove the selection indicator and close or reset the relevant information panel.
- Clicking empty ground must not leave stale selection state.
- Clicking another selectable object must select or inspect that object according to the interaction rules.
- Clicking a building opens its contextual information panel.
- Clicking a resource deposit should show its type, remaining resources and relevant interactions.
- Clicking wildlife should show its species and appropriate state where useful.
- Clicking decorative scenery should behave consistently.

Do not accidentally deselect a unit when the player is issuing a movement command. Use an explicit and understandable interaction state model.

## 6.3 Movement and work commands

On desktop:

- Right-clicking valid terrain should issue a move command to the selected unit.
- Workers should be able to receive appropriate gather, farm, build or work commands.
- Add Stop, Hold Position and other relevant commands where suitable.
- Add patrol or combat commands for appropriate military units.

On Android:

- Provide equivalent visible touch controls.
- Use clear contextual buttons or command modes instead of requiring right-click.
- Ensure that movement, selection, camera panning, zooming and building placement do not conflict.
- Keep touch targets large enough to use comfortably on a phone or tablet.

Only show commands that are valid for the selected entity.

## 6.4 Reliable pointer and touch handling

- Distinguish a click from a camera-drag gesture using a sensible movement threshold.
- Prevent dragging the camera from accidentally selecting units.
- Prevent a tap from triggering duplicate actions through overlapping mouse, pointer and touch handlers.
- Convert pointer coordinates correctly under camera translation and zoom.
- Use reliable hitboxes for units, buildings, resources and animals.
- Handle overlapping isometric objects consistently.
- Ensure selection works at different zoom levels.
- Prevent accidental map interaction when using HUD panels or menus.

Optional desktop improvements include box selection, useful keyboard shortcuts, double-click selection of nearby units of the same type and Escape-key behaviour.

Implement optional controls only when they can coexist cleanly with the core interaction model.

